ion x' kangaroo  court style guidance on campus sexual  assaults in addition to the two-for-one  executive order Trump has also required  agencies to impose an aggregate  regulatory cost each year of zero  the neverending growth of red tape in  America has come to a sudden screeching  and beautiful halt we're not just you  know going to eliminate some small  insignificant rules but we also have to  have a negative regulatory cost budget  you know which is what we had for 2017  and we went even below that and I think  it's the first time since at least we've  been keeping records that there's been a  year like that you know we're also  encouraging the agencies to look at  deregulation not just from the numbers  side you know many agencies are peeling  back guidance documents or streamlining  paperwork burdens and all of that I  think is having an impact the  free-market enthusiasts at the  Competitive Enterprise Institute a  Washington DC think-tank have been  tallying new regulations annually for  the past 25 years in 2017 they found the  administration cut the number of pages  in the Federal Register by more than 1/3  and the 3281 new rules amounted to the  lowest count since records began being  kept in the mid-1970s I haven't seen  personally anything like the regulatory  reductions that have taken place the  Competitive Enterprise Institute slide  Wayne Cruz is already calling Donald  Trump the least regulatory president of  all Trump has gone after saying for  example the waters of the United States  rule EPA so-called waters of the United  States rule is one of the worst examples  of federal regulation it's welcoming to  see the FCC turn back the so-called net  neutrality rule I'm impressed and  unimpressed at the same time I love what  has happened and I want it to continue  but to really address all of that you've  got to get Congress to act in other  words the executive branch can rearrange  the scope and process of enforcement but  only legislators can rep